would have been better without his tedious remarks on women. Personally, I'm a pretty huge fan of the Christian theologian Thomas Altizer, who borrows from Nietzsche and Hegel","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thinking as I do that the Creator of this World is a very Cruel Being &amp; being a Worshipper of Christ, I cannot help saying: &quot;the Son, O how unlike the Father!&quot; First God Almighty comes with a Thump on the Head. Then Jesus Christ comes with a balm to heal it. - William Blake, Christian mystic and visionary","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/774-pilgrim","name":"Pilgrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar774_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T18:59:29-08:00","id":307731,"title":"","content":"Something I remembered I had; <i>Looking into Nietzsche... I'm not fond of his writings but some things are pertinent.<br /> <br /> Nietzsche's relationship with science was ambivalent. While he recognized its utility and praised its naturalism, he also regarded science as being based on faith.<br /> <br /> &quot;To make it possible for this discipline to begin, must there not be some prior conviction...? We see that science also rests on faith; there simply is no science &quot;without presuppositions.&quot;<br /> Nietzsche then continues:<br /> &quot;... from where [does] science [take] its unconditional faith or conviction on which it rests, that truth is more important than any other thing, including every other conviction?... I will not deceive, not even myself; and with that we stand on moral ground.</i><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T00:18:27-08:00","id":307944,"title":"","content":"Science is not based on faith because otherwise scientific method would not work. Scientists may assume certain things and create hypotheses, but a hypothesis must be tested to become a theory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T04:28:50-08:00","id":308039,"title":"","content":"I do love Nietzsche, although it became almost a clich\u00e9 liking his work. The first book I read, curiously enough, was &quot;The Birth of Tragedy&quot; where he thoroughly explores Greek theatre, culture and the reflection of the gods as symbols. Only from that did I moved on to the more well known and popular works. He is an amazing author and TRULY HATE when people try to connect him with Nazism.<br /> <br /> Keeping on the germans (to me they are the best philosophers alongside the classical greeks) I also love to read Kant, despite reading a whole book being an act of courage in itself. But his take on the morals is simply amazing and his logic unsettling. I also like the neo-Kantians like Kelsen a lot, I had to study and produce an oral essay on his work The Pure Theory of Law while attending University.<br /> <br /> Despite not being religious the works of <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Saint Augustine are worth a read and study. On the french side the Political Testament of Cardinal </span></span>Richelieu is a favourite (usually I dislike french philosophers, boring and seldom to I agree with anything). The Ethics of Espinoza also are worth reading.<br /> <br /> And from the top of my mind these are the philosophers that most call out to me, not mentioning the classical greeks of course (Socrates, Plato, Sofocles, Esquilo, etc.).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2435-prophet","name":"Prophet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2435_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}